# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `.env` is gitignored; never commit real keys.

# ---- LLM backend: openrouter | ollama | echo ----
SKYRAG_LLM_BACKEND=openrouter

# ---- OpenRouter ----
# Get a key at https://openrouter.ai/keys
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-or-v1-REPLACE_ME
# Optional attribution headers (shown on OpenRouter leaderboards)
OPENROUTER_REFERER=
OPENROUTER_TITLE=SKYRAG
# Pin which upstream provider OpenRouter routes to -- a serving company's *slug*,
# NOT a backend name and NOT the model's author namespace. `meta-llama` in
# `meta-llama/llama-3.1-70b-instruct` says who published the weights; the provider
# is whose GPUs run them. There is no `meta-llama` provider, and the `meta` one
# does not serve these models.
#
# Left blank, routing is automatic and each run records that it was automatic. Pin
# it for a benchmark, for three reasons that are not interchangeable:
#   1. `seed` support is provider-dependent, so determinism is not guaranteed while
#      routing is free -- and measured on 2026-08-18 it is not achieved even so:
#      the same prompt twice reproduced 82.5% of its own recommendations.
#   2. latency and price differ between providers serving the same model, so free
#      routing puts routing luck into the cost axis.
#   3. **quantization differs too.** DeepInfra serves both Llama 3.1 models at fp8,
#      CoreWeave at bf16. Unpinned, two rows of the same arm can be served at
#      different numeric precision -- a confound inside "the 70B" that no column
#      would record.
#
# The pin is global: it applies to every arm, so it must be a provider that serves
# *every* model in the sweep. As of 2026-08-19 only `deepinfra` and `coreweave`
# serve both llama-3.1-70b-instruct and llama-3.1-8b-instruct. Do NOT use `novita`
# (16,384 ctx) or `cloudflare` (32,000) -- both are below what a full retrieval
# builds and would reintroduce the context overflow closed on 2026-08-18.
#
# **The pin is `deepinfra` at fp8 (D6, 2026-08-20; re-confirmed 2026-08-26).** Both
# Llama 3.1 models are served at fp8, and both are cheaper there by 2-11x.
#
# This template previously pinned `coreweave`/bf16 and argued for it below, on the
# ground that quantization degrades small models more than large ones, so fp8 could
# widen or narrow the 8B-vs-70B gap this experiment exists to measure -- bf16
# removing the variable rather than balancing it. That argument was correct in
# principle and was **settled empirically against**, twice:
#   * P1 (2026-08-25) measured the reproducibility floor at BOTH precisions back to
#     back: bf16 91%, fp8 72%. The floor does not transfer between them, so the
#     precision must be pinned -- but a lower floor is not by itself disqualifying.
#   * P1-wide (2026-08-26) asked the question that decides it: can the instrument
#     still RESOLVE a treatment effect at fp8? At 20 paired queries per condition it
#     resolves the context-trim effect at both precisions (bf16 36.3 pts, p=2.1e-03;
#     fp8 17.7 pts, p=4.7e-03). fp8 attenuates the effect to roughly half its bf16
#     size but does not hide it. **Verdict: STAY AT fp8** (commitment 3.3e).
#
# Two consequences travel with this pin and are recorded so they are not
# rediscovered: fp8 halves apparent effect sizes, which is why the equivalence
# margin was set at the tight end of its defensible range (DEC-11/C12); and every
# fp8 run is discarded by a later move to bf16, which is why the precision had to
# settle before L5's repeats were bought.
#
# Do NOT use `novita` (16,384 ctx) or `cloudflare` (32,000) -- both are below what a
# full retrieval builds and would reintroduce the context overflow closed on
# 2026-08-18.
#
# Note this is NOT a fidelity-to-source argument: the original local setup ran
# Ollama, whose Llama 3.1 tags are 4-bit by default, so bf16 is further from it
# than fp8 is. Check the roster is still as described on the day you run:
#   curl -s https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/models/meta-llama/llama-3.1-70b-instruct/endpoints
# Fallbacks are disabled when this is set, so a wrong value fails loudly rather
# than quietly serving from somewhere else.
SKYRAG_OPENROUTER_PROVIDER=deepinfra

# Models use OpenRouter's `vendor/model` naming.
# The paper used llama3.1:70b locally; this is the hosted equivalent.
SKYRAG_MODEL=meta-llama/llama-3.1-70b-instruct
# Optional: cheaper models for the two auxiliary calls, to cut cost.
SKYRAG_KEYWORD_MODEL=meta-llama/llama-3.1-8b-instruct
SKYRAG_EXPANSION_MODEL=meta-llama/llama-3.1-8b-instruct

# ---- Ollama (only used when SKYRAG_LLM_BACKEND=ollama) ----
OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434

# ---- Generation ----
SKYRAG_TEMPERATURE=0.0
SKYRAG_MAX_TOKENS=4096
# One fixed seed, shared by every arm -- that is what fairness requires, and it
# is what the methods section will state. Set the variable to an empty value (or
# `none`/`null`/`off`) to request an *unseeded* run; leaving it out entirely
# takes the 34 below. Absent and empty mean different things here on purpose.
#
# Two caveats belong with any determinism claim, both recorded in
# project_knowledge/task_log/backlog_2026-08-18_events_seed-determinism-and-repeat-semantics.md:
# upstream providers honour `seed` on a best-effort basis, and at
# SKYRAG_TEMPERATURE=0.0 decoding is greedy, so the seed has almost nothing to
# steer in the first place.
SKYRAG_SEED=34

# ---- Embedding (always runs locally; OpenRouter has no embedding API) ----
SKYRAG_EMBEDDING_MODEL=all-mpnet-base-v2
SKYRAG_DEVICE=auto
SKYRAG_BATCH_SIZE=32

# ---- Retrieval ----
SKYRAG_PER_KEYWORD=7
SKYRAG_TOP_K=20
# Backstop on how many courses may reach a generation prompt, applied after the
# round-robin merge. Deliberately ABOVE what the settings above can produce
# (SKYRAG_MAX_KEYWORDS x SKYRAG_PER_KEYWORD x platforms = 560), so it binds on no
# arm. That is the point: a cap that binds on one arm and not another is a
# confound, because truncating the context is itself a treatment. Lower it only
# deliberately -- a run where it bound reports `context_courses_dropped > 0`.
# Empty, `none` or `off` removes the backstop entirely.
SKYRAG_MAX_CONTEXT_COURSES=600
